(vector (map (:registry/operating-states (vector :intake :propose :approve :execute :audit)), (:registry/required-technologies (vector :robotics :identity :audit-ledger)), (:actor/scope-excluded-ops (vector :finalize-handicraft-item :finalize-craft-execution-decision :declare-product-quality-cleared :declare-safety-clearance :override-shop-safety-officer-judgment :override-safety-officer-judgment)), (:actor/robotics? true), (:adr/related (vector "2799007211-cloud-itonami-isco-7211-metal-moulders-coremakers-coverage" "2799007317-cloud-itonami-isco-7317-handicraft-workers-wood-basketry-coverage" "2799007311-cloud-itonami-isco-7311-precision-instrument-makers-coverage")), (:adr/status :accepted), (:actor/scope-excluded-terms (vector "finalize the handicraft item" "finalize the craft execution decision" "proceed with the craft execution" "authorize the craft execution" "declare the product quality-cleared" "declare the product quality cleared" "declare the item safety-cleared" "declare the item safety cleared" "declare safety-cleared" "declare quality-cleared" "override the shop safety officer's judgment" "override the safety officer's judgment" "override shop safety officer judgment")), (:adr/id 2799007319), (:phase/rollout "{0 {:label \"read-only\", :writes #{}, :auto #{}}, 1 {:label \"assisted-logging\", :writes #{:log-work-record}, :auto #{}}, 2 {:label \"assisted-coordination\", :writes #{:schedule-crew-operation :coordinate-supply-order :log-work-record}, :auto #{}}, 3 {:label \"supervised-auto\", :writes #{:flag-safety-concern :schedule-crew-operation :coordinate-supply-order :log-work-record}, :auto #{:schedule-crew-operation :coordinate-supply-order :log-work-record}}}"), (:actor/domain :manufacturing/handicraft-workshop-scheduling-logistics-coordination), (:actor/proposal-ops (vector :log-work-record :schedule-crew-operation :flag-safety-concern :coordinate-supply-order)), (:actor/hard-checks (vector :artisan-verified :workshop-verified :effect-propose :op-not-allowed :scope-exclusion)), (:registry/maturity :implemented), (:actor/isco-08 "7319"), (:actor/always-escalate-ops (vector :flag-safety-concern)), (:adr/doc_type "adr"), (:operation/nodes (vector :intake :advise :govern :decide :request-approval :commit :hold)), (:operation/entry-point :intake), (:adr/body "# ADR 2799007319: cloud-itonami ISCO-08 7319 Handicraft Workers Not Elsewhere Classified workshop scheduling/logistics coordination coverage\n\n## Status\n\nAccepted\n\n## Context\n\nThis is part of the occupation-classification track (sibling to the\n`cloud-itonami-isic-*` industry-actor rollout, same governing\nADR-2607121000): codify every ISCO-08 occupation unit group as an\nautonomous \"actor\" (LLM/advisor behind an independent Governor, a\n`langgraph.graph` StateGraph, append-only audit ledger), modeled on a\nrobotics premise -- a robot performs the occupation's physical/\nadministrative work, proposing actions to an independent Governor that\ngates them, never dispatches hardware itself, always escalates\nhigh-stakes decisions to a human. This ADR covers **ISCO-08 class 7319\n-- Handicraft Workers Not Elsewhere Classified** only, Wave3\n(production/trades) of the rollout.\n\nThe `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's live `{:id \"7319\" ...}` entry\nwas verified against a fresh clone before any work began (`clojure -M\n-e \"(require 'kotoba.occupation) (println (kotoba.occupation/get-\noccupation \\\"7319\\\"))\"` from a checkout with the `kotoba-lang/\ntechnology` sibling present at the correct `../technology` relative\npath), and its `:name` read exactly \"Handicraft Workers Not Elsewhere\nClassified\" -- confirming the premise, no mismatch. Prior to this ADR\nthe entry's `:maturity` was `:spec` with `:required-technologies\n[:robotics :identity :audit-ledger]` and no `:repo`/`:business-id`; no\nrepository existed at `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7319` (`gh\napi` 404 confirmed before scaffolding). This ADR and its companion\nregistry edit promote the `7319` entry to `:implemented`.\n\nISCO-08 7319 is a broad, generic residual \"Not Elsewhere Classified\"\ncategory covering diverse handicraft work (varied hand-tools, varied\nmaterials) not captured by any more specific ISCO craft code (e.g.\n7311-7318, 7321-7323). Standard workshop safety hazards apply\ngenerically (hand-tool injury, varied-material handling) without a\nsingle dominant hazard type, unlike e.g. 7211's heat/molten-metal\nhazard or 7317's cut/dust/repetitive-strain hazard triad -- so this\nactor's hazard-escalation vocabulary is deliberately generic\n(hand-tool-hazard / material-handling-hazard / equipment-condition)\nrather than technique-specific. The controlling constraint for this\nvertical is that the closed op allowlist must NEVER include any op\nthat directly finalizes a craft-execution decision (e.g. deciding a\nhandicraft item is finished) or a product-quality/safety-clearance\ndecision (e.g. declaring an item quality- or safety-cleared), nor\noverride a shop safety officer's judgment -- those decisions must\nalways be a hard permanent block, never auto-commit-eligible. This\nactor is deliberately scoped to WORKSHOP SCHEDULING/LOGISTICS\nCOORDINATION ONLY -- it never performs handicraft work or makes\nquality/safety-clearance decisions itself.\n\nThe reference implementation mirrored closely for module SHAPE and\nthe closed-allowlist + independently-verified-provenance safety\npattern is\n[`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7211`](https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7211)\n(Metal Moulders and Coremakers: `store`/`advisor`/`governor`/`actor`\nsplit, `langgraph.graph` StateGraph, full doc set, already landed with\na closely-related generic-workshop-craft safety pattern) -- cloned and\nread in full in this session. Its `deps.edn` `langgraph` git-sha pin\nwas reused verbatim for this repo. The adjacent registry entry `{:id\n\"7317\" ...}` (Handicraft Workers in Wood, Basketry and Related\nMaterials, already `:implemented`) was inspected for naming\ndisambiguation and vocabulary precedent only -- 7319's own domain\ndesign in this task is deliberately more generic (no single dominant\nmaterial/technique/hazard) than 7317's wood/basketry-specific one, so\nthe op-allowlist, scope-exclusion phrasing and hazard vocabulary here\nstay residual-category-generic rather than borrowing 7317's\nwood/basketry-specific terms verbatim.\n\n## Decision\n\nPublish `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7319` as an OSS workshop\nscheduling/logistics-coordination actor for generic handicraft\nworkshops, following the SAME governed-actor architecture as every\nprior `cloud-itonami-isco-*` actor in this fleet: **Craft Coordination\nAdvisor ⊣ CraftCoordGovernor** on a `langgraph.graph` StateGraph\n(`io.github.kotoba-lang/langgraph`, git-sha-pinned identically to the\nreference's `deps.edn` -- a different library coordinate from the\n`langgraph-clj` used by the unrelated `20-actors/*` actor family),\nwith an independent Governor, human-in-the-loop approval via\n`interrupt-before`, and an append-only audit ledger.\n\nThis actor is deliberately **workshop scheduling/logistics\ncoordination ONLY, never direct craft-execution or\nquality/safety-clearance authority** -- it must NEVER be designed to\nexercise, simulate exercising, or propose exercising a\ncraft-execution decision (e.g. deciding a handicraft item is\nfinished), a product-quality/safety-clearance decision (e.g. declaring\nan item quality- or safety-cleared), or any override of a shop safety\nofficer's judgment. These are not merely gated behind escalation --\nthey are structurally absent from the op-allowlist entirely.\n\n### Closed proposal-op allowlist (all `:effect :propose`)\n\n- `:log-work-record` -- job/commission/progress data logging\n- `:schedule-crew-operation` -- crew/task scheduling proposal\n- `:flag-safety-concern` -- surface a hand-tool-hazard/\n  material-handling/equipment-condition concern, ALWAYS escalates,\n  NEVER auto-commit eligible\n- `:coordinate-supply-order` -- craft-materials procurement proposal\n\nThis actor has **NO op, anywhere in its allowlist, that resembles** a\ncraft-execution decision, a product-quality/safety-clearance decision,\nor a shop-safety-officer-judgment override. These are **structurally\nabsent from the op-allowlist entirely**, not merely gated behind\nescalation -- an op such as `:finalize-handicraft-item` cannot even be\nproposed by the reference mock advisor (its `infer`/`rationale-for`\nfunctions only ever emit one of the four allowlist ops), and if any\ncode path (a forged proposal, a future LLM advisor, a misbehaving\nfork) ever produced such an op, the governor's closed-allowlist HARD\ncheck (`:unknown-op`) rejects it unconditionally and permanently. A\nsecond, independent HARD check (`:scope-excluded-action`) names six\nconcretely-forbidden ops (`craftcoord.governor/scope-excluded-ops`:\n`:finalize-handicraft-item`, `:finalize-craft-execution-decision`,\n`:declare-product-quality-cleared`, `:declare-safety-clearance`,\n`:override-shop-safety-officer-judgment`,\n`:override-safety-officer-judgment`) as defense-in-depth, and is\nverified by dedicated unit tests\n(`craftcoord.governor-test/hard-on-scope-excluded-op-finalize-handicraft-item`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-finalize-craft-execution-decision`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-declare-product-quality-cleared`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-declare-safety-clearance`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-override-shop-safety-officer-judgment`),\nplus a full-graph-run regression\n(`craftcoord.actor-test/holds-a-scope-excluded-op-even-at-high-confidence`,\n`.../holds-a-quality-safety-clearance-op-even-at-high-confidence`).\n\n### Four HARD governor checks (permanent, un-overridable)\n\n1. **Artisan unregistered** -- any proposal must resolve to an\n   independently registered crew-member record (`:no-artisan`). Never\n   trusts the proposal's own claim of who the artisan is.\n2. **Workshop unregistered** -- any proposal must cite an\n   independently registered workshop (`:no-workshop`). Together with\n   check 1, this directly implements the CRITICAL invariant that a\n   worker/workshop record must be independently verified/registered\n   before any action.\n3. **Effect not `:propose`** -- any other `:effect` value is a HARD\n   block (a claim to directly actuate outside governance)\n   (`:no-actuation`).\n4. **Op not allowed / scope-excluded action** -- any `:op` outside the\n   closed four-op allowlist is a HARD, PERMANENT block (`:unknown-op`),\n   and independently, any proposal whose `:op` names one of the six\n   concretely-forbidden ops above, OR whose free-text `:rationale`\n   names a finalization/execution action for finalizing a craft item,\n   declaring a product quality- or safety-clearance, or overriding a\n   shop safety officer's judgment, is a second HARD, PERMANENT block\n   (`:scope-excluded-action`), evaluated unconditionally on content --\n   defense-in-depth on top of the allowlist check: even if a future\n   change somehow widened the allowlist, content-based rejection still\n   independently blocks craft-execution/quality-clearance/\n   safety-override language wherever it appears in free text.\n\n### One always-escalate (SOFT) gate, one threshold gate, plus the general confidence floor\n\n- `:flag-safety-concern` -- ALWAYS escalates to a human, regardless of\n  confidence (governor's implicit always-risky check). This is the\n  ONLY channel by which the robot may surface a hand-tool-hazard/\n  material-handling/equipment-condition observation -- it never\n  resolves the concern itself, and the robot's role ends at \"here is\n  the hazard data,\" never \"here is what I recommend doing about shop\n  safety.\"\n- a `:coordinate-supply-order` proposal whose `:cost` exceeds\n  `supply-cost-threshold` (2000, inclusive boundary verified by\n  `ok-supply-order-at-threshold-boundary`) -- always needs human\n  sign-off, independent of confidence. This is an ESCALATION, not a\n  hard block, per the task's explicit domain design: an over-threshold\n  craft-materials order is not itself unsafe, unlike a\n  craft-execution/quality-clearance/safety-officer-override attempt,\n  which is why it is handled by a different mechanism (soft escalate\n  vs. structural absence).\n- (advisor confidence below `confidence-floor` 0.6 also escalates, for\n  any op.)\n\n### Self-trip guard (fleet-known bug class, carried over from the sibling ISIC/ISCO tracks)\n\nA governor's scope-exclusion term list phrased as a bare noun (e.g.\n\"craft\", \"quality\", \"safety\") can accidentally match inside the mock\nadvisor's own default rationale text for a legitimate proposal,\nself-blocking the actor's own happy path -- this is exactly the risk\nCLAUDE.md's worked example warns about for this task.\n`craftcoord.governor/scope-excluded-phrases` is phrased exclusively as\nfinalization/execution ACTIONS (e.g. \"finalize the handicraft item\",\n\"declare the product quality-cleared\", \"override the shop safety\nofficer's judgment\" -- matching the task's own worked example\nphrasing verbatim), never bare nouns, and\n`craftcoord.governor-test/default-mock-advisor-proposals-never-self-trip-on-scope-exclusion`\nis a dedicated regression test asserting every op in the closed\nallowlist, driven through the default mock advisor, never trips\n`:scope-excluded-action`. The mock advisor's own default rationale\ntemplate was deliberately written to contain the bare nouns (\"logged\nwork record for artisan ...\", \"scheduled crew operation for\nhandicraft task ...\", \"... routed for shop safety officer review\")\nWITHOUT containing any of the full finalization-action phrases, so the\nregression test is a genuine stress test of the bare-noun-vs-\naction-phrase distinction, not a vacuous pass. Green on first run in\nthis session -- the scope-exclusion self-trip guard itself was never\ntripped during development.\n`craftcoord.actor-test/holds-a-scope-excluded-op-even-at-high-confidence`\nand `.../holds-a-quality-safety-clearance-op-even-at-high-confidence`\nadditionally exercise the same invariant through the full graph run,\nconfirming a scope-excluded proposal never reaches `commit-record!`\nregardless of disposition path.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's `{:id \"7319\" ...}` entry\n  promoted from `:spec` to `:implemented`, with `:repo`/`:business-id`\n  set to the real repository\n  (`https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7319` /\n  `cloud-itonami-isco-7319`), and `maturity-summary`'s implemented\n  count bumped accordingly (recomputed live from the registry via\n  `kotoba.occupation/maturity-summary`, not assumed, immediately\n  before the edit).\n- Full test suite (`clojure -M:test`): 24 tests, 52 assertions, 0\n  failures, 0 errors -- raw output captured in this ADR's\n  `:test/counts` and in the task report, independently re-verified\n  against a brand-new fresh clone of the pushed repository (not just\n  the local working copy).\n- No `manifest/west.yml` changes -- this actor is registered purely via\n  the `kotoba-lang/occupation` capability registry, consistent with\n  sibling `cloud-itonami-isco-*`/`cloud-itonami-isic-*` actors.\n- No `kotoba-lang/technology` dependency was added to the actor's\n  `deps.edn` -- only the central `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry\n  itself depends on it, to resolve `:required-technologies` stacks.\n- This ADR is EDN-only, matching the live `90-docs/adr` convention\n  (commit `6d2d0eeda658`, \"Remove all .md, normalize ADRs to\n  tx-data\") -- a single-entity EDN vector with an `:adr/body` string\n  blob rather than a paired `.md`+`.edn`.\n\n## Alternatives considered\n\n- Giving the actor an op like `:approve-craft-execution` or\n  `:approve-quality-clearance` that is merely gated behind an\n  always-escalate list (the shape `cloud-itonami-isco-3313` uses for\n  `:approve-over-ceiling-posting`): rejected outright per the task's\n  explicit domain-design guidance -- an always-escalate op still means\n  the *capability* to finalize a craft-execution or\n  quality/safety-clearance decision exists in the code path and is\n  merely deferred to a human gate. For a decision this severe (hand-\n  tool injury, varied-material handling, product-quality/safety\n  clearance), the capability itself must not exist. The only op\n  related to shop-safety escalation is `:flag-safety-concern`, which\n  surfaces the need for human/shop-safety-officer attention and never\n  itself decides, authorizes, or defers a craft-execution or\n  quality/safety-clearance decision for later automatic execution.\n- Making the supply-cost ceiling a HARD block (mirroring the 3313\n  reference's transaction-amount-ceiling HARD check): rejected per the\n  task's explicit domain design -- an over-threshold craft-materials\n  order is not itself unsafe, so it is handled as a soft escalate\n  gate, not a hard block; only the craft-execution/quality-clearance/\n  safety-officer-override class is a hard, unconditional block.\n- A bare-noun scope-exclusion term list (e.g. `[\"craft\" \"quality\"\n  \"safety\"]`): rejected outright per the fleet-known self-trip bug\n  class described above (and CLAUDE.md's own worked example for this\n  exact task), and per the explicit instruction to phrase\n  scope-exclusion terms as finalization/execution ACTIONS, never bare\n  nouns -- this actor's own domain necessarily discusses\n  craft/quality/safety-officer concepts to do its scheduling/logging\n  job, so a bare-noun list would risk self-blocking the actor's own\n  legitimate proposals (regression-tested directly against rationale\n  text that deliberately contains those bare nouns).\n- Adopting a single dominant hazard type (e.g. modeling 7211's heat-\n  exposure-risk or 7317's cut/dust/repetitive-strain triad verbatim):\n  rejected -- 7319 is an explicitly broad/generic residual\n  \"Not Elsewhere Classified\" category with varied hand-tools and\n  varied materials and no single dominant hazard type per the task's\n  CRITICAL guidance, so `:hazard-type` stays a free-form field with\n  generic examples (hand-tool-hazard / material-handling-hazard /\n  equipment-condition) rather than a single hardcoded dominant hazard.\n- Merging `artisan` and `workshop` into a single entity (only checking\n  one provenance record): rejected -- the two-entity provenance shape\n  (independently-registered artisan AND independently-registered\n  workshop) matches the CRITICAL invariant that a \"worker/workshop\n  record must be independently verified/registered before any action\"\n  and the established pattern from prior `cloud-itonami-isco-*` actors\n  with two-sided provenance checks.\n\n## Itemized safety confirmation\n\nEvery op in this actor's closed proposal-op allowlist, and why it is\nsafe under the constraints above:\n\n1. **`:log-work-record`** -- job/commission/progress data logging\n   only. Requires an independently registered artisan AND workshop\n   (`:no-artisan`/`:no-workshop`). Not a craft-execution or\n   quality/safety-clearance decision in any respect -- it is a\n   records-management action.\n2. **`:schedule-crew-operation`** -- crew/task scheduling proposal\n   only. Cannot carry a craft-execution decision (structurally absent\n   from the op's field shape; any such attempt via free text is\n   caught by the `:scope-excluded-action` content check). Does not\n   authorize, finalize, or draw any conclusion about a specific\n   handicraft item -- it is a calendar/logistics action.\n3. **`:flag-safety-concern`** -- surfaces a hand-tool-hazard/\n   material-handling/equipment-condition concern to a human/shop\n   safety officer; ALWAYS escalates, unconditionally, at every\n   confidence level, NEVER auto-commit eligible. This is the ONLY\n   channel through which a safety observation may be surfaced, and it\n   explicitly never resolves, decides, or acts on anything itself --\n   the shop safety officer reviews and acts entirely on their own\n   authority.\n4. **`:coordinate-supply-order`** -- craft-materials procurement\n   proposal only (item, cost). Not a craft-execution or quality/\n   safety-clearance decision of any kind -- it is a\n   purchasing-logistics action, gated by ordinary cost-ceiling\n   escalation (above 2000 always escalates).\n\nNo op in this allowlist, and no op anywhere in this codebase, resembles\na craft-execution decision (e.g. deciding a handicraft item is\nfinished), a product-quality/safety-clearance decision (e.g. declaring\nan item quality- or safety-cleared), or a shop-safety-officer-judgment\noverride -- these categories are structurally absent, not gated,\nconfirmed by: (a) the closed four-op allowlist itself\n(`craftcoord.governor/allowed-ops`), (b) six concretely-named\nforbidden-op fixtures rejected at the governor-unit level\n(`craftcoord.governor/scope-excluded-ops`,\n`craftcoord.governor-test/hard-on-scope-excluded-op-finalize-handicraft-item`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-finalize-craft-execution-decision`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-declare-product-quality-cleared`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-declare-safety-clearance`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-override-shop-safety-officer-judgment`),\nand (c) a content-based scope-exclusion check independent of the `:op`\nfield (`craftcoord.governor/scope-excluded-phrases`,\n`.../contains-excluded-phrase?`), tested directly against a rationale\nthat attempts to finalize a handicraft item, declare a product\nquality-cleared, or override a shop safety officer's judgment even via\nan otherwise-allowlisted op\n(`craftcoord.governor-test/hard-on-scope-excluded-rationale-even-with-allowlisted-op`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-rationale-declare-quality-cleared`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-rationale-override-shop-safety-officer`).\n"), (:adr/date "2026-07-18"), (:actor/cost-threshold-escalate-ops "{:coordinate-supply-order 2000}"), (:test/counts "{:total 24, :assertions 52, :failures 0, :errors 0}"), (:actor/coordination? true), (:actor/name "Handicraft Workshop Scheduling and Logistics Coordination Practice"), (:db/id -1), (:test/coverage (vector :governor-hard-checks :advisor-shapes :integration :store-contract :scope-exclusion-self-trip-regression :artisan-verification :workshop-verification :closed-op-allowlist :supply-cost-threshold-escalation)), (:registry/business-id "cloud-itonami-isco-7319"), (:registry/repo "https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7319"), (:adr/scope (vector :kotoba-lang/occupation :cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7319)), (:test/simulator :craftcoord.actor-test), (:adr/title "cloud-itonami-isco-7319 — Handicraft Workers Not Elsewhere Classified Workshop Scheduling/Logistics Coordination Coverage"), (:adr/source-format "edn"), (:adr/references (vector 2607121000)), (:actor/id :cloud-itonami-isco-7319)))
